A slice holds a pointer into a backing array, plus len and cap. When you slice a large array/slice, that pointer still points inside the big array — so even though the small slice only 'sees' a few elements, the whole backing array stays pinned in memory as long as that slice is alive, since the garbage collector can't free something still referenced. That's the leak: actual memory usage is much bigger than len suggests.


s := make([]int, 0)
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		s = append(s, i)
		fmt.Printf("len=%d cap=%d\n", len(s), cap(s))
	}

notesMap.2026-08-05:
Implicitly and structurally: the compiler checks whether the type's method set contains every method the interface requires, with identical name, parameters, and return values. There's no explicit 'implements' declaration — satisfaction is automatic and checked at compile time. And since a pointer's method set includes both value and pointer receiver methods, while a value's method set only includes value receiver methods, that can determine whether it's T or *T that actually satisfies the interface.

type shape interface {
	area() float64
}

type circle struct {
	radius float64
}

func (c circle) area() float64 {
	// ...
}

func printShapeInfo(s shape) {
	c, ok := s.(circle)
	if ok {
		radius := c.radius
		fmt.Printf("s is a circle, radius: %v\n", radius)
		return
	}
}

func printNumericValue(num interface{}) {
	switch v := num.(type) {
	case int:
		fmt.Printf("%T\n", v)
	case string:
		fmt.Printf("%T\n", v)
	default:
		fmt.Printf("%T\n", v)
	}
}

Por que um método com receiver ponteiro não pertence ao method set do valor?
Because a pointer can always be safely dereferenced to access the value, but the reverse isn't guaranteed — not every value is addressable (like values inside a map). To keep a simple, exception-free rule, Go defines that a pointer's method set includes both value and pointer receiver methods, while a value's method set only includes value receiver methods. This directly affects interface satisfaction: a type with pointer receiver methods only satisfies an interface through the pointer

